l kit on the kaia cube the  custom protocol can be requested through  our apps lab  and we are working on standardizing and  releasing the all prep power viral also  as a standard  kayak cube process  and that brings me to the end of my  slides and here i'd like to just leave  off with another excerpt from a paper by  dr ahmed  um  concluding that extraction of  lysate using the rnez power microbiome  and kai keep connect platform to obtain  a high quality rna  is highly suitable for wastewater  analysis thank you for your attention  and  i'll hand it over to colin for the  additional pcr portion thank you  thanks dominic now i'm going to tell you  guys how you can use the kai acuity  digital pcr system to detect sars kovi 2  in your wastewater samples  i'd just like to reiterate a few things  that dominic has already said about sars  cov2 and wastewater testing before going  any further  the real advantage of using wastewater  samples to monitor sars kobe 2 is that  within a single sample of wastewater  hundreds thousands and even millions of  people are represented and this allows  researchers to get a much better  snapshot of the community spread of sars  kobe 2 when compared to looking at  individual clinical samples and this  data from wastewater can be used as an  early warning sign for outbreaks and  allows researchers to get a snapshot of  sars cov2varian spread within a single  sample  and they can relate the quantities of  each variant to each other very easily  now digital pcr is particularly well  suited to this  task because unlike qpcr digital pcr is  absolute quantification where no  standard curve is required allowing data  collected from between various sites to  be compared with great certainty and  precision owing to the fact that the  data is absolute in its quantification  additionally because digital pcr  quantitates a little bit differently  from qpcr  the chemistry is a little bit more  robust to  the wastewater samples which are known  to contain  pcr inhibitors  so why is the kai acuity nanoplate  digital pcr system ideal for wastewater  testing  first of all the time to result with the  nanoplate is only two hours which is  much shorter than the five hours it  takes using the conventional digital  droplet pcr system from bio-rad  second  the kai acuity nano plate system is  capable of high multiplexing up to five  plex  meaning that users can detect multiple  sars cov2 variants within a single  reaction well  lastly the kai acuity nanoplate system  is fully integrated  in its workflow and offers users the  flexibility  of throughput and resolution  the plates  are very similar  to qpcr plates in their shape and well  layout and the 24 and 96 well plate  options allow users  the flexibility of throughput and  resolution all at once  i'd like to take a brief moment to point  out the features of the kai acuity  nanoplates and how they differ from the  digital droplet pcr workflow  in the kayaking nanoplate you can see  your results literally in the raw image  of each and every well on your nanoplate  second of all  sealed nanoplates prevent contamination  between wells once your reaction mixes  in a l it's in a closed environment and  it stays there  solid partitions compared to the  droplets have a very reproducible size  and therefore are particularly well  suited for the poisson calculations  that underlie digital pcr which rely on  a distinct and discrete partition volume  because we can image an entire well at  once and not via the flow cytometry  style of digital droplet pcr the readout  is fast  and because our plates are the  standardized format of all  pcr plates  the workflow is very simple and anyone  who's familiar with qpcr will have no  problem jumping in to the kayaking  nanoplate world  of course in order to use the kai acuity  nanoplates you need kai acuity  instruments and here i'd like to talk  about the four instruments that we have  available  we have a single plate instrument called  the kai acuity one which comes in two  configurations there's the two channel  instrument or the five channel  instrument  next to that we have two higher  throughput instruments called the kaia  q84 and the kaia q8 which can process  four or eight plates respectively in  parallel  and both of those instruments come only  in one configuration with respect to  multiplexing and that's the five channel  system  on this slide we have the consumables  from kyogen which you can use to detect  sars kovi 2 and wastewater as mentioned  before cayegian offers nanoplates in a  variety of formats  26k nanoplates contain 26 000 partitions  per well allowing users to load up to 28  microliters of sample per 40 microliter  reaction  alternatively users can opt for the  lower resolution 8.5 k nano plate which  comes in two formats 24 well and 96 well  or 8 500 partitions per well  are found with reaction volumes of 12  microliters where users can use up to  seven microliters of sample per reaction  we have a kai acuity one-step viral  rt-pcr kit developed special for cyrus  cov2 detection it comes in one size  enough for 520 reactions in 26k  nanoplates  and the kit contains the 4x master mix  100x multiplex reverse transcription mix  and rnas free water it's not available  on web shop ordering but if you're  interested contact your kayak and sales  specialists to order  it for the one step viral rt pcr kit we  came up with distinct single and  multi-plate workflows  for users interested in only processing  a single plate at a time  we came up with a single plate workflow  which works on all kai acuity  instruments  as is part of all kai acuity workflows  users assemble their reaction mixes in  so-called pre-plates  and after they've set up their reactions  in pre-plates they transfer the reaction  mix to a nanoplate seal the nanoplate  and then put the nanoplate into the kai  acuity instrument  where there rt incubation and two-step  kai acuity pcr cycling take place  without any additional user input  now  for users interested in processing  multiple plates in parallel using the  kai acuity 4 and 8 instruments we came  up with a dedicated multi-plate workflow  owing to the fact that reverse  transcriptases are not meant to be  sitting around for the length of time  that it would require if one were to  load a full  8 plate or 4 plate instrument and wait  up to hours until the reverse  transcription step in the cycling  program took place this is particularly  important for the generation of  non-specific signal or spurious  side products which you don't want  happening at room temperature therefore  we came up with the idea that users  would assemble  their reaction mixes in pre-plates which  are anyways 96 well pcr plates standard  and they will perform an offline reverse  transcription step in the pre-plate  after the reverse transcription  took place  the user would inactivate the reverse  transcriptase enzyme using a 10-minute  inactivation step then transfer their  reaction mix to the nanoplate and then  perform the two-step kai acuity pcr  cycling in the kai acuity instrument  now we'd like to show you guys some  application data where we quantified the  abundance of sars kovi ii using rna  extracted from german wastewater using  the methods that dominic described  previously  in the powerpoint  so here below we have nine distinct  biological samples which were taken from  the same pool of collected wastewater  and were then extracted using  mce membrane  or the concentration of retin-tot  through amicon filtration devices or  centrifugation of wastewater to form  pellets which were then extracted  we assayed the rna using the n1 and n2  assays developed for  sars cov2 by the cdc we use five  microliters  of  rna in  40 microliter 26k nanoplate reactions  and the original eluent was derived from  50 milliliters of wastewater  as you can see consistent with what  dominic saw the pellet and amicon retin  tot samples quantified sars cov2 better  than the mce membrane  we'd also like to show you guys some  application data where we tried out some  troubleshooting of techniques when  dealing with wastewater samples because  they can be tricky at times  and so we played with three variables  first we increased the annealing  extension time from 30 seconds at 60  degrees to 90 seconds at 60 degrees in  our two-step pcr system  second we investigated whether using  less eluit per digital pcr reaction  improved the quantification overall  lastly  we tried adding a well-known pcr  inhibitor  magic pill psa to see if that would also  help and as you can see using the n1  assay  using less template  increasing the extension time from 30  seconds to 90 seconds and adding bsa to  the system  all helped  in increasing the detection of sars cov2  in our wastewater samples so i would  just like to recommend that if anybody's  having difficulty with their wastewater  samples try playing with those three  variables  last but not least  we've also gathered from our experience  in quantifying sars kobe 2 in wastewater  that users coming from the qpcr world to  the digital pcr world are sometimes  surprised that their quantification  expectations aren't matched when they  come to digital pcr  and we found out that many times it's  because  concentration expectations determined  with qpcr are determined using standards  which are inaccurately quantified  here we have four  single stranded rna standards  which were placed into digital pcr  reactions and expected concentration of  a thousand copies per microliter and as  you can see  all four standards came below  the expected concentration and most  importantly among the standards there  were different concentrations  indicating that  unique features to each standard um were  off each concentration for each standard  was off sometimes by a factor of three  which would be quite significant for  important quantification experiments  so here is kaijin's solution for sars  kobe 2 detection in wastewater using  extraction methods which we developed  especially for  this task and the kai acuity instrument  and the kai acuity one step viral rtpcr  kit that i just talked about  so uh thanks for your attention and i  will be taking your questions right now
 

